but overkill. Let's assume floating point equality is acceptable for typical cases.\n\nNow implementation steps in Python:\n\n1. Extract lengths, scores, budget from case.\n2. n = len(lengths)\n3. Compute DP for exact token counts: dp_exact[b] = max score achievable with exactly b tokens. Initialize dp_exact with -inf for all b from 0 to budget. dp_exact[0] = 0. Also store for each b the best tuple? Not needed initially.\n   For each i in range(n):\n        len_i = lengths[i]\n        score_i = scores[i]\n        for b in range(budget, len_i-1, -1):\n            if dp_exact[b-len_i] is not -inf:\n                new_score = dp_exact[b-len_i] + score_i\n                if new_score > dp_exact[b] + 1e-12: # with tolerance\n                    dp_exact[b] = new_score\n   After loop, find max_score = max(dp_exact). Then find b_min = min(b for b in range(budget+1) if abs(dp_exact[b] - max_score) < 1e-12). We'll use a small epsilon.\n\n4. Now we need to reconstruct the lexicographically smallest subset (in terms of indices) that achieves total length b_min and total score max_score. We'll build a suffix DP that stores for each position i and exact token count w the maximum score achievable from i to end. But we only need to know if a certain score is reachable. Since we have max_score and b_min, we can do forward greedy with a suffix DP that stores reachable (score, tokens) pairs? That would be many.\n\nAlternate simpler: We can incorporate tie-breaking during the original DP by storing for each b the best (score, tokens, list_of_indices) and compare using lexicographic order: first compare score (higher better), then tokens (lower better), then list (lexicographic smaller better). Since tokens is always equal to b (since we store exact tokens), the second criterion becomes b. But we only store exact b, so if for a given b we have multiple ways to achieve same score, we need to store the one with lexicographically smallest indices. So during DP update, when we compute new