function must not mutate case. Use only standard library.\n\nNow produce the final answer: a JSON object with key \"implementation\" containing the Python source string.\n\nWe need to ensure the source is properly escaped. Write the code inside a string.\n\nWe'll define solve(case) that reads case['lengths'], case['scores'], case['budget'].\n\nWe need to handle float equality carefully. Use a tolerance.\n\nPseudo:\ndef solve(case):\n    lengths = case['lengths']\n    scores = case['scores']\n    budget = case['budget']\n    n = len(lengths)\n    INF_NEG = float('-inf')\n    dp = [ (INF_NEG, []) for _ in range(budget+1) ]\n    dp[0] = (0.0, [])\n    eps = 1e-9\n    for i in range(n):\n        l = lengths[i]\n        s = scores[i]\n        for b in range(budget, l-1, -1):\n            prev_score, prev_indices = dp[b-l]\n            if prev_score is INF_NEG:\n                continue\n            new_score = prev_score + s\n            cur_score, cur_indices = dp[b]\n            if cur_score is INF_NEG or new_score > cur_score + eps:\n                dp[b] = (new_score, prev_indices + [i])\n            elif abs(new_score - cur_score) < eps:\n                new_indices = prev_indices + [i]\n                if new_indices < cur_indices: # lexicographic\n                    dp[b] = (new_score, new_indices)\n    best_score = INF_NEG\n    best_tokens = None\n    best_indices = []\n    for b in range(budget+1):\n        score, indices = dp[b]\n        if score is INF_NEG:\n            continue\n        if score > best_score + eps:\n            best_score = score\n            best_tokens = b\n            best_indices = indices\n        elif abs(score - best_score) < eps:\n            if b < best_tokens:\n                best_tokens = b\n                best_indices = indices\n            elif b == best_tokens and indices < best_indices:\n                best_indices = indices\n    return {\n        \"selected_indices\": best_indices,\n        \"tokens_used\": best_tokens,\n        \"total_score\": best_score\n    }\n\nBut note: total_score should be a number (float). Return as is.\n\nAlso: if best_tokens is None (no feasible?), but dp[0] always exists, so best_tokens=0, best_score=0.\n\nNow we need to write this as a Python string inside JSON. Use triple quotes? JSON string cannot contain literal newlines, we must escape newlines as \\n and backslashes as \\\\ etc. We'll format the code as a single line or properly escaped. For readability, we can write the code with newlines and then escape them. We need to ensure no unescaped characters.\n\nLet's produce the final JSON.\n\nWe'll output:\n{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    lengths = case['lengths']\\n    scores = case['scores']\\n    budget = case['budget']\\n    n = len(lengths)\\n    INF_NEG = float('-inf')\\n    dp = [(INF_NEG, []) for _ in range(budget+1)]\\n    dp[0] = (0.0, [])\\n    eps = 1e-9\\n    for i in range(n):\\n        l = lengths[i]\\n        s = scores[i]\\n        for b in range(budget, l-1, -1):\\n            prev_score, prev_indices = dp[b-l]\\n            if prev_score is INF_NEG:\\n                continue\\n            new_score = prev_score + s\\n            cur_score, cur_indices = dp[b]\\n            if cur_score is INF_NEG or new_score > cur_score + eps:\\n                dp[b] = (new_score, prev_indices + [i])\\n            elif abs(new_score - cur_score) < eps:\\n                new_indices = prev_indices + [i]\\n                if new_indices < cur_indices:\\n                    dp[b] = (new_score, new_indices)\\n    best_score = INF_NEG\\n    best_tokens = None\\n    best_indices = []\\n    for b in range(budget+1):\\n        score, indices = dp[b]\\n        if score is INF_NEG:\\n            continue\\n        if score > best_score + eps:\\n            best_score = score\\n            best_tokens = b\\n            best_indices = indices\\n        elif abs(score - best_score) < eps:\\n            if b < best_tokens:\\n                best_tokens = b\\n                best_indices = indices\\n            elif b == best_tokens and indices < best_indices:\\n                best_indices = indices\\n    return {\\n        \\\"selected_indices\\\": best_indices,\\n        \\\"tokens_used\\\": best_tokens,\\n        \\\"total_score\\\": best_score\\n    }\"\n}\n\nWe need to make sure to escape double quotes inside the string. The code uses single quotes for dict keys, but we have double quotes in the return dict. We should escape those double quotes with backslash. Also, the string itself is enclosed in double quotes, so any inner double quotes must be escaped. But we used single quotes for keys in code, but the return dict uses double quotes. We can change return dict to use single quotes? The output should be a Python dict that will be serialized to JSON later. The spec says \"return a JSON-serializable object\". So returning a Python dict with keys as strings is fine. In the code, we can use single quotes for keys in the returned dict to avoid escaping. But the JSON output of the solve function will be converted by Python's json.dumps? Actually the evaluator expects the solve function to return something that can be JSON-serialized. So returning a dict with single-quote keys is fine; when serialized, they become double quotes. So we can write the return dict with single quotes to simplify escaping. Let's do that.\n\nSo code: return { 'selected_indices': best_indices, 'tokens_used': best_tokens, 'total_score': best_score }\n\nThen no inner double quotes. Also ensure that the string itself is properly escaped: newlines as \\n, backslashes as \\\\ (we have none). We also need to escape single quotes if any inside the string, but we used double quotes for the outer JSON string, so single quotes are fine.\n\nLet's produce final answer."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":1134,"completion_tokens":5534,"total_tokens":6668,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":1024},"completio